Z||000000||Tom88gt||12-22-2000||09:20 PM||||New solenoid.  New vaccum lines.  Rodney's steel vac line kit.  I check the electrical connections they seem normal.  Code 32 always hits when driving 65+.  Read all the old posts.<P>Can a cracked EGR tube (tube between manifold and valve) cause "32"?  I don't see how as the vac switch on the solenoid only monitors solenoid vaccum.<P>What are good electrical checks?  Both solenoid and vac switch wires get 12V with ignition on.  Both ground wires have good continuity.  The solenoid has 90 ohms continuity.<P>IM GOING CRAZY!!!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Black88GT||12-22-2000||10:53 PM||||What about your exhaust crossover?<P>Your EGR tube will cause that if its cracked.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||3
Z||000002||mshill||12-23-2000||02:30 AM||||Tom,  I had the exact same issue with mine last year, just after my rebuild.  I had originally put my cracked EGR tube back on to my rebuild after trying to fix it with JB Weld.  It worked ok for a while then I started to get 32s again.  I took off the tube and it had hairline crackes in the JB Weld so I replaced it wit one from the boneyard.  Five thousand miles later and have not had an engine code since.<P>I think that it is likely that even the smallest vacuum leak at higher extended RPMs puts a higher demand on the vacuum system and evena small leak (like in the EGR tube) will eventually trigger the code 32.<P>BTW,  I have started scavenging EGR tubes off of every Camaro/Firebird 2.8 that I see cause they will fit if you slot the plenum side bolt holes.  The way they are built, you can never have enough replacements.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||12-24-2000||01:06 PM||||But how is the vaccum side of the EGR valve and the EGR tube side of the EGR valve connected?  As long as the vaccum side is not leaking, how can it matter if the tube is cracked, or even missing?  <P>The EGR valve opens fully when I put a vaccum on the line.  But slowly closes if I just hold the vaccum and don't keep applying more vaccum.  I think the bellow might have a pinhole leak.  But I thought the EGR solenoid vaccum switch only needed a couple inches (very small) vaccum to close and tell the ECM everything's OK?||||reg||5
Z||000004||terryk||12-24-2000||03:26 PM||||At idle, is the switch closed (i.e. is pin D8 at 0V or dang close to it?)<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||12-24-2000||08:19 PM||||I think I found my problem.  No vaccum at the line coming from the throttle body.  Used a T connector and used vaccum port on upper intake, by the distributor as a vaccum souce.  Drove my regular route.  No check engine light (which used to happen EVERY damn time).  I'm guessing the vaccum port under the throttle body is totally plugged up with gunk and **** .  I guess I'm spending Xmas day pulling the throttle body off, unless there is a way to clean out that port with it on the car.||||reg||11
Z||000006||terryk||12-24-2000||08:42 PM||||Good find. Take the TB off and clean it. Remove the IAC and TPS. Clean it thoroughly.<P>TK||||reg||1
